---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';

// Hiro's own list, 2026-08-05, in his order — not a prototype, unlike the
// albums array this replaced. Don't reorder it alphabetically or by layer:
// the sequence is his and it currently runs roughly framework → language →
// styling → framework → fundamentals → design → AI → vcs → cloud.
//
// FLAT, no categories (his call, same day). It was grouped for a few hours
// under the argument that a bare list of names sitting directly beneath the
// interests list would read as more of the same list. That risk is real and
// is now handled by LAYOUT rather than by grouping — see the grid note in
// the section below.
//
// SEVEN since 2026-08-05: HTML/CSS and AWS came out. Both failed the same
// test — altitude. Everything left is a named product; those two were the
// substrate and a category. HTML/CSS is assumed of anyone qualified to be
// reading the page, and Tailwind already implies the CSS; AWS is ~200
// services and the bare word claims either too much or nothing. Don't add
// either back without a specific reason, and if a cloud returns, name the
// service.
//
// `icon` names an entry in Icon.astro, and `key` names its colour token
// (--tool-<key> in global.css). Adding a tool needs BOTH: a drawing that
// survives a 24-unit box at 2px, and a free hue — the palette has eight and
// green is spoken for by the wordmark, so the next tool is the tight one.
const tools = [
	{ name: 'Astro', icon: 'astro' as const, key: 'astro' },
	{ name: 'JavaScript', icon: 'javascript' as const, key: 'js' },
	{ name: 'Tailwind', icon: 'tailwind' as const, key: 'tailwind' },
	{ name: 'React', icon: 'react' as const, key: 'react' },
	{ name: 'Figma', icon: 'figma' as const, key: 'figma' },
	{ name: 'Claude Code', icon: 'claude' as const, key: 'claude' },
	{ name: 'Git', icon: 'git' as const, key: 'git' },
];
---

<Layout title="About — Hiro Eernisse" description="About Hiro Eernisse, design engineer.">
	<Nav />

	<main class="mx-auto max-w-[960px] px-5 py-12 sm:py-16">
		{/* Side by side again from lg, after one day stacked. The row died on
		    08-02 when the page came in to 960px: the figure held 26rem of that,
		    which left the prose ~346px — about FIVE words a line at the 21.4px
		    it was set in. Both of those numbers moved on Hiro's ask, and the row
		    came back with them. As of 08-03 the prose is 16.625px and the figure
		    is 16rem, so the text column takes 561px at lg — still well inside
		    --measure's 665px, which is the "narrower" Hiro asked for and 1.7× the
		    width that killed the row.

		    THE ROW IS A FIXED SUM and that's the thing to understand before
		    touching any of it: 561 + 47.5 gap + 304 figure = main's 912.5px
		    exactly. The card cannot get wider without the figure or the gap
		    getting smaller — there is no slack anywhere else, and widening main
		    is not local to this page (the footer, the home page and the project
		    pages all share the 960px cap).

		    17rem → 16rem on 08-05, when Hiro asked for a slightly wider card; the
		    19px the photo gave up went straight to the column. That is the same
		    trade made on 08-03 in the other direction, when the photo came down
		    19rem → 17rem and the 2rem went to the prose. The gap was left alone
		    both times: 47.5px is the rhythm the sections use inside the card too,
		    and it is the cheaper thing to protect.

		    Watch the em count here rather than the pixels: the CARD is 561px but
		    the text inside it is 511.5px after px-5, which is 30.8× the font size
		    and ~62 characters — inside the usual 45–75. This is the figure to
		    check before shrinking the photo or the type again, because the column
		    has less room to give than the pixel width suggests.

		    Below lg there is no room for two columns at any of these numbers, so
		    it still stacks and the prose keeps its own 28rem cap — close enough
		    to the row's 542px that the two states read as one measure rather
		    than two.

		    NOT --measure: that token is the site's reading cap, shared with the
		    home hero and the project pages, and this column is narrower than it
		    for a layout reason that doesn't apply to them. */}
		{/* PLAIN col below lg (Hiro, 08-05): the photo sits UNDER the card. This
		    reverses flex-col-reverse, which stood from 08-03 on the argument that
		    the stack should open on the photo the way the lg row's eye does, and
		    that ending the page on the tallest unlabelled block was worse.

		    What changed is that the block below the fold is no longer the
		    photo — it's the card, which now holds every word on the page. Opening
		    the stack on a 380px image meant a phone visitor scrolled past the
		    whole picture before reading who this is. Card first puts the greeting
		    in the first screen at every width, and the photo becomes the thing
		    you arrive at rather than the thing you get past.

		    It also makes DOM order, visual order and reveal order the same
		    sequence for the first time — card, then figure, at both widths. The
		    reversal used to invert the middle one, which is why the old note had
		    to explain that the photo revealed first while sitting last. Nothing
		    needs explaining now; delete this paragraph if the layout ever stops
		    being that simple. */}
		{/* ── items-START, and the whole column is one card (Hiro, 08-05) ──
		    Every piece of text on the page now lives in this card, beside the
		    photo, and the card's top edge lines up with the photo's. Three
		    changes that are really one: the sections came back INTO the column,
		    the column became a card, and the row went back to top-aligned.

		    They had to move together. Bottom-alignment (08-04, one day) was only
		    ever meaningful while the column held two blocks — greeting and prose
		    — whose last line could sit on the caption's. With interests and the
		    toolbox back in here the column runs far taller than the figure, so
		    there is no shared bottom left to align to and items-end would simply
		    have pushed the photo down to meet a card that ends 600px below it.
		    Top-aligned is the only edge the two still share.

		    This also spends the 08-04 argument that took the sections OUT of the
		    column for a full-width rule. The rules are now card-width and bleed
		    to its edges instead — see the sections. The page is no longer ruled
		    into bands; the CARD is, which is the same device at a smaller scale
		    and is what "contained in the card" means.

		    Nothing here is a measured offset, which is the one thing worth
		    keeping from the version this replaces: the row has been a measured
		    margin, then a bottom alignment, and is now a top alignment, and only
		    the first of those needed a number that could go stale. */}
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-12 lg:flex-row lg:items-start lg:gap-10">
			{/* ── The card is ONE reveal, and the page's first (Hiro, 08-05) ──
			    .reveal sits here rather than on the pieces inside it, so the whole
			    card — greeting, prose, both sections — fades and slides in as a
			    single object. Every child that used to carry its own .reveal (the
			    h1, the two paragraphs, both sections at --base 700ms and --i 0–3)
			    had it REMOVED, and that removal is required, not tidying: a
			    .reveal inside a .reveal animates opacity against a parent already
			    animating opacity, so the children arrive twice and the card's own
			    fade multiplies against theirs.

			    The cascade this replaces staggered five elements over 1150ms and
			    read as the page assembling itself. One object arriving reads as
			    the page BEING there — which is the right note for a card that is
			    now the entire content of the page.

			    --i: 0 with no --base, so it opens the cascade at 0ms. The figure
			    follows at --i: 1 — the standard 150ms stagger, matched to the
			    home page's hero→grid beat; see the note there for how a bespoke
			    450ms gap fell out of step with the hairline finale. */}
			{/* ── NO CARD BELOW sm (Hiro, 2026-08-15) ── Every card class below is
			    gated behind sm:, so on a phone this is plain prose sitting on the
			    page ground and the card materialises at 640px.

			    The reason is DOUBLE INSET, and it is arithmetic rather than taste.
			    main carries px-5 and the card carried px-5 inside it, so the text
			    paid 23.75px twice on each side. Measured on a 390px phone: main is
			    342.5px and the card left 295px of text — 17.7× the 16.625px type,
			    about 35 characters a line, against 511.5px and ~62ch for the same
			    paragraph at lg. The frame was taking 16% of an already-short measure
			    and putting it under the 45-character floor the row note holds
			    everything else to. Plain, the column is the full 342.5 and ~41ch,
			    and the bio paragraph sets in four lines instead of five.

			    NOT an orphan fix, which is the plausible wrong reason to read into
			    this. "I'm a Design Engineer based in the San Francisco Bay Area."
			    is 489px and wraps to two lines at both widths — the sentence that
			    drove px-5 in the first place is indifferent here. The gain is
			    measure across the whole card, not one line break.

			    sm (640px) is where the arithmetic stops biting, not a round number.
			    The card is capped at 28rem = 532px, so from ~600px of main upward
			    the cap binds before the viewport does and the second inset comes out
			    of slack instead of out of the measure; below that the cap is inert
			    and the two paddings compound. If either px-5 moves, re-derive this
			    breakpoint rather than assuming it.

			    THE PADDING GOES WITH THE BORDER, all four sides, and that pairing is
			    the whole fix — keeping the inset while dropping the frame would
			    spend the measure on nothing visible. Vertical too: main's py-12
			    (57px) already opens the page, so the greeting needs no second lid.

			    What SURVIVES the gate, deliberately: max-w-[28rem], which is the
			    stacked prose cap and predates the card (see the row note); and both
			    .card-rule sections, which are drawn on the sections themselves and
			    now span the full content column. That is the page-ruled-into-bands
			    device the 08-05 note calls this card a smaller-scale copy of — at
			    phone width it simply reverts to the original scale, which is why
			    losing the frame costs the sections nothing.

			    The figure below is untouched. An image is its own object and reads
			    as framed at any width; a paragraph does not need a frame to read as
			    a paragraph.

			    rounded-[var(--radius)] + --line + bg-clip-padding is the site's card
			    material, the same one the photo beside it and the project cards
			    wear, so the two halves of the row read as one set of parts. The
			    clip is load-bearing rather than decorative: --line is translucent,
			    so without it --surface paints THROUGH the border and the card's
			    edge lands a different colour than the photo's identical one.

			    ASYMMETRIC PADDING, and the asymmetry is the point (Hiro, 08-05):
			    px-5 (23.75px) and py-10 (47.5px). The two axes are answering
			    different asks and pull opposite ways — he wanted more room top and
			    bottom, and the fix for the orphan below is more room left and
			    right, which only the horizontal axis can give.

			    px-5 was set by ONE SENTENCE and is no longer tight. "I'm a Design
			    Engineer based in the San Francisco Bay Area." measures 489px at
			    16.625px. Against the old 542px column, p-8 (38px) left 464 and
			    broke it after "Bay", p-6 (28.5px) left 483 and still broke it by
			    six pixels, and px-5 left 492.5 — fitting with 3.5px to spare, which
			    was the whole margin.

			    The card went to 561px on 08-05 and that margin is now 22.5px: the
			    text is 511.5px against the sentence's 489. Comfortable rather than
			    lucky, and the fragility flagged the day before is gone. px-5 stays
			    anyway — it is a sensible inset in its own right, and re-widening it
			    would spend the room that was just bought.

			    text-pretty on the prose is still there and still the safety net —
			    see the note. It has more slack to protect now, not less reason to
			    exist.

			    THE VERTICAL PADDING IS NOT SYMMETRIC EITHER, and the asymmetry is
			    what makes it LOOK symmetric: pt-10 (47.5px) against pb-[56px]. It
			    was py-10 until 08-05, a clean 47.5 both ends that measured 60.1px
			    of air above the greeting's cap and 51.7 below the last list item —
			    an 8.4px lean the eye reads as a top-heavy card.

			    The cause is half-leading, and it is the same one the greeting's
			    mb note records. The h1's line box carries +12.6px above its cap;
			    the last li's carries +4.2px below its baseline. Equal padding
			    therefore paints unequal. 56 - 47.5 = 8.5 ≈ the 8.4 difference,
			    which is why the bottom number looks arbitrary and isn't.

			    Both edges now read ~60px. Adding to the BOTTOM rather than cutting
			    the top was deliberate: Hiro asked for more vertical room on 08-05
			    and taking it off the top would have quietly walked that back.

			    It reads as roughly 2:1 against the horizontal, which looks wrong
			    written down and correct on the page — the eye measures a text
			    block's top and bottom air against its LINE HEIGHT (27px), not
			    against its side margins. */}
			<div class="reveal max-w-[28rem] sm:rounded-[var(--radius)] sm:border sm:border-[var(--line)] sm:bg-[var(--surface)] sm:bg-clip-padding sm:px-5 sm:pb-[56px] sm:pt-10 lg:min-w-0 lg:max-w-none lg:flex-1" style="--i: 0">
				{/* text-3xl, two steps ABOVE the section headings (Hiro, 08-05, in two
				    asks the same day — text-xl → 2xl → 3xl). It
				    sat at their exact size from 08-02, on the argument that a
				    greeting isn't a page title and the column should read as two
				    equal blocks under one photo. That argument is now half-spent:
				    the second block moved out of this column on 08-04, so there is
				    no pair left to keep equal, and the greeting is the only heading
				    the row contains. 35.625px — back at the 3xl it was cut from on
				    08-02, though for a different reason: that cut was about the
				    column reading as two equal blocks, and there is only one block
				    in the column now. The masthead still carries the name at full
				    size on every page, so this stays a greeting and 4xl is still
				    the line not to cross.

				    mb-8 (38px). It went mb-4 → 8 → 10 → and back to 8 on 08-05, and
				    the last move is the one with a reason worth keeping.

				    SET OPTICALLY, NOT GEOMETRICALLY. At mb-10 the greeting sat 60.3px
				    of ink-to-ink from its prose while the card's top padding put it
				    60.1px from the card's edge — dead centre between the two, which
				    reads as a heading belonging to neither. A heading has to be
				    nearer what it heads than what is above it. mb-8 gives 50.8px
				    against the edge's 60.1 and the affinity lands.

				    The geometric number is NOT the gap. This h1's line box carries
				    +12.6px of half-leading above its cap and only +3.6px below its
				    baseline, so the 38px here paints as 50.8 downward and the 47.5
				    above it paints as 60.1. Measure ink, not boxes, before retuning
				    anything in this card — the same asymmetry is why the section
				    rules needed 40px below and 47.5 above to look centred.

				    The wave scales with all of this for free — .greeting-wave is
				    sized in em, so it tracks the h1 without a second number. It is
				    decorative and aria-hidden, or it reads out as "waving hand" in
				    the middle of a Japanese greeting. */}
				<h1 class="mb-8 text-3xl [font-weight:var(--weight-emph)] tracking-tight text-[var(--ink-heading)]">
					{/* lang="ja" (2026-08-18): the document is lang="en", so without this a
					    screen reader reads these five kana with an English voice — the first
					    thing on the page, and unintelligible. The span wraps the RUN, not the
					    heading: the wave beside it is aria-hidden and the h1 carries nothing
					    else, but tagging the heading would claim the whole line is Japanese
					    and would be wrong the moment anything English joins it. */}
					<span lang="ja">はじめまして。</span><span aria-hidden="true" class="greeting-wave"><Icon name="wave" /></span>
				</h1>
				{/* text-sm (16.625px), down from base (19px) on 08-03 — Hiro's ask,
				    applied to the body copy only. The two h2s and the greeting stay
				    at text-xl, so the step from heading to prose widened, which is
				    what makes the headings read as headings now rather than as bold
				    body text.

				    space-y-5 (23.75px) is rem-based and so did NOT move with the
				    type. Against the old 30.875px leading it was 0.77×; against the
				    new 27px it is 0.88× — still a paragraph break rather than a
				    section one, because the rules and the 95px gap below now carry
				    that job unambiguously. Don't tighten this to restore 0.77×: the
				    ratio was a proxy for "reads as one block", and the divider makes
				    the proxy unnecessary.
				    The h1 keeps mb-4 rather than opening to match: its 19px is
				    deliberately equal to the section headings' mt-4 (see above), and
				    that symmetry is worth more than closing the gap from the other
				    side. */}
				{/* text-pretty (2026-08-05): the browser avoids leaving a single short
				    word alone on a paragraph's last line, rebalancing the lines above
				    it instead. It is doing NOTHING today — px-5 fits the first
				    sentence on one line with 3.5px to spare — and that is exactly why
				    it's here. That 3.5px is one copy edit or one type-scale nudge from
				    gone, and the failure it prevents is the one Hiro flagged: "Area."
				    alone on line two. Without this, losing the margin means an orphan;
				    with it, losing the margin means two balanced lines. */}
				<div class="space-y-5 text-pretty text-sm leading-relaxed">
					<p>I'm a Design Engineer based in the San Francisco Bay Area.</p>
					<p>
						I build with AI, but I make the decisions that matter myself. Taste, and knowing how a
						person moves through a product, is still human work.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Started as the bio's closing line — "Outside of work, I play heavy
				    metal drums and hike," a sentence doing a list's job at the end
				    of a paragraph run — and was promoted to a section on 08-02,
				    then filled out to four entries the same day. A real heading, NOT
				    the small-bold uppercase kicker this codebase has dropped twice
				    (see the project page). It takes the reveal slot the removed line
				    held, so the cascade is unchanged.

				    The marks are decoration on top of words that already say the
				    same thing, so they're aria-hidden: a screen reader would
				    otherwise hear "Playing drums drum".

				    Leading the row (Hiro, 08-02), so each one gets a fixed slot
				    rather than riding on its own advance width — that's what puts
				    the LABELS on one left edge, which is the only reason to hang
				    icons off the front of a list. The slot used to be an explicit
				    1.25em because emoji advance widths differ per platform; drawn
				    icons share one 24-unit box, so the slot is now intrinsic and
				    .interest-mark just states the size it was always going to be.

				    SVG since 2026-08-04, on Hiro's ask, replacing 🥁👾🎬🥾 — see the
				    block in Icon.astro for what the emoji couldn't do and why each
				    one redraws its OWN emoji rather than a neighbouring idea. A
				    hue each, from --mark-*; the values,
				    their ratios, and how this squares with the page declining the
				    accent on its headings are all at .interest-mark in global.css.

				    Ruled off the block above it on 08-03. Balanced on purpose: an
				    unbalanced rule reads as belonging to whichever block it sits
				    nearer, and this one belongs to neither.

				    mt-10 (47.5px) above, pt-[40px] below — UNEQUAL NUMBERS FOR AN
				    EQUAL LOOK, and don't "fix" them back to a matching pair. It was
				    47.5/47.5 until 08-05, which is balanced in the box model and
				    visibly off in ink: 51.7px above the rule against 59.3 below, so
				    every rule sat ~8px nearer the block above it and did exactly what
				    this note says it must not. The h2 under each rule carries +10.8px
				    of half-leading above its cap, where the paragraph or list item
				    above ends only +4.2px after its last ink. 40 + 10.8 = 50.8
				    against 47.5 + 4.2 = 51.7, and the rule finally sits centred.

				    Total is now ~87.5px against the old 57px, which is still the
				    "more space between sections" half of the 08-03 ask.

				    --line-soft, not --line: --line is what the photo draws its edge in
				    (and, until 08-05, the album tiles), and a divider that matched it
				    would read as another frame rather than as punctuation between
				    blocks. It is now the PEAK of that colour rather than all of it —
				    .card-rule (08-05) paints the --rule gradient these are drawn with
				    everywhere else on the site, so the ends fade out the way the nav's
				    edge and the projects rule do. Only the paint changed; every number
				    in this note survived it, which is the reason .card-rule stayed a
				    border instead of becoming a pseudo-element. (It briefly became
				    one on 08-06 to join the page dividers' load finale, and was
				    reverted the same day — these rules live on a card that enters as
				    one object, so they enter with it. The .card-rule comment in
				    global.css has both arguments; read it before restructuring.)
				    A border on the section rather than an <hr>, because the h2s
				    already mark the divisions structurally — this is presentation on
				    top of semantics that exist, not a substitute for them.

				    THE RULE'S WIDTH HAS BEEN FOUR THINGS IN THREE DAYS, so read the
				    whole arc before moving it a fifth time. ~542px to 08-04, when it
				    sat inside the prose column. Then the full 912.5px, when Hiro
				    asked for a divider that spanned the page and the section moved
				    out to become a direct child of main. Then card-width, when the
				    section came back into the column (08-05) and the column became a
				    card. Now CONTENT-width, same day, Hiro's call: the rule stops
				    where the prose stops instead of bleeding to the card's inner
				    edges.

				    Which is why there is no -mx-5/px-5 pair here any more. That pair
				    was what "contained in the card" used to mean — the negative margin
				    cancelled the card's px-5 so the BORDER reached both inner edges
				    and the matching padding put the content back. Plain sections now,
				    so the border simply spans the content box and needs nothing to
				    track the card's padding; if that padding moves again, this follows
				    it for free, which the old pair never did (it chased px-5 through
				    8 → 6 → 5 in a single day).

				    The argument the old pair was answering is still on the record and
				    was not refuted, only overruled: a rule inset from its container
				    can read as an underline under the heading above it rather than as
				    a division. What makes ~23.75px survivable where 38px did not is
				    that the rule is now flush with the prose on BOTH edges — it lines
				    up with something, rather than floating inside the card at a width
				    nothing else shares. If it ever does start reading as an underline,
				    that is the thing that broke, and the fix is the bleed coming back.

				    What survives all four: the rule spans a column the page already
				    uses, never an arbitrary width. The page was ruled into bands; the
				    card is now ruled into bands. Same device, smaller scale.

				    Still NOT full-bleed to the window, and that constraint is
				    untouched by any of this. The nav's hairline is the site's only
				    edge-to-edge rule (e69bfec), which is what makes it read as the
				    bar's own edge rather than as content. */}
				<section class="card-rule mt-10 pt-[40px]">
					<h2 class="text-xl [font-weight:var(--weight-emph)] tracking-tight text-[var(--ink-heading)]">Personal Interests</h2>
					{/* items-start, not items-baseline: an SVG's baseline is its bottom
					    margin edge, so items-baseline would hang all four marks off the
					    text baseline rather than sitting them on the line. .interest-mark
					    carries the offset that centres them instead. */}
					<ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-sm leading-relaxed">
						<li class="flex items-start gap-3">
							<span aria-hidden="true" class="interest-mark interest-mark--drums">
								<Icon name="drums" />
							</span>
							<span>Playing drums</span>
						</li>
						<li class="flex items-start gap-3">
							<span aria-hidden="true" class="interest-mark interest-mark--invader">
								<Icon name="invader" />
							</span>
							<span>Video game design &amp; pixel art</span>
						</li>
						<li class="flex items-start gap-3">
							<span aria-hidden="true" class="interest-mark interest-mark--clapper">
								<Icon name="clapper" />
							</span>
							<span>Tokusatsu (Godzilla, Ultraman, etc.)</span>
						</li>
						<li class="flex items-start gap-3">
							<span aria-hidden="true" class="interest-mark interest-mark--boot">
								<Icon name="boot" />
							</span>
							<span>Enjoying nature and hiking</span>
						</li>
					</ul>
				</section>

				{/* ── Toolbox (2026-08-05) ── Took the slot the albums grid vacated the
				    same day, and inherits its rule: mt-10/pt-10 either side of a
				    .card-rule, the full 912.5px, --i: 3 so the reveal cascade
				    is unchanged. Three ruled bands on the page, one device.

				    It IS an icon-and-label list, same as interests directly above it,
				    and as of 08-05 also the same SHAPE — one vertical column, Hiro's
				    call. Both devices that used to hold the two apart are now spent:
				    the grid went with this change, and the monochrome went when the
				    tools took hues earlier the same day.

				    What still separates them, in the order it does the work: the 95px
				    and the full-width rule between the sections; two h2s that name
				    different things; and the LINE RHYTHM, which is the quiet one —
				    interests are phrases ("Video game design & pixel art") and tools
				    are single product names, so the two blocks have visibly different
				    ragged edges even set identically. That last one is why this reads
				    as two lists rather than one run of eleven.

				    Worth knowing this argument has now been had three times: the
				    albums section (08-02) was covers rather than titles for it, this
				    section was a dl and then a grid for it, and it is now a plain
				    list. If the two blocks ever do start reading as one, the fix is
				    the rule and the gap between them, not the list itself.

				    Not tags/chips, which is the obvious answer and a trap: they were
				    on the project pages and came OFF on 07-29 (see the .btn note in
				    global.css). --surface's comment still says "cards, tags, nav
				    buttons" and that middle term is now stale. Reintroducing the
				    device here would quietly undo a removal, on a page that isn't
				    where the argument happened. */}
				<section class="card-rule mt-10 pt-[40px]">
					<h2 class="text-xl [font-weight:var(--weight-emph)] tracking-tight text-[var(--ink-heading)]">Toolbox</h2>
					{/* One column at every width (Hiro, 08-05). It was 3 × 3 column-major
					    and then 4 × 2 row-major inside a day; both are gone, and with
					    them the grid's job of holding this block apart from the
					    interests list — see the section note for what carries that now.

					    space-y-2 and the same li as interests, deliberately identical
					    rather than nearly so: two lists set to the same rhythm read as
					    two deliberate lists, where two set ALMOST the same read as one
					    list with a glitch in it. If these diverge again, diverge on
					    purpose and by more than a step. */}
					<ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-sm leading-relaxed">
						{tools.map((tool) => (
							<li class="flex items-start gap-3">
								<span aria-hidden="true" class={`tool-mark tool-mark--${tool.key}`}>
									<Icon name={tool.icon} />
								</span>
								<span>{tool.name}</span>
							</li>
						))}
					</ul>
				</section>
			</div>
			{/* Fixed 16rem at lg so the flex-1 column above resolves to a known
			    width (see the note there); it is the last thing in the row, so
			    shrink-0 keeps the prose taking the slack rather than the photo.
			    Below lg the row is gone and the old stacked caps stand.

			    Every number here came down a notch on 08-03 (Hiro: "slightly
			    smaller"): 19rem → 17rem at lg, 18/22rem → 16/20rem stacked, and
			    the caption with it — 14.25px → 13.3px, icon 3.5 → 3. The caption
			    shrank alongside the photo rather than holding still, because it is
			    measured against the image it sits under, not against the prose; a
			    fixed caption under a smaller photo would have grown in relative
			    terms and started competing with the body copy that just got
			    smaller itself.

			    17rem → 16rem on 08-05, for a wider card rather than a smaller
			    photo — the row is a fixed sum and the column had nowhere else to
			    take it from. The caption did NOT move this time, deliberately:
			    08-03 cut the photo 10.5% and the caption went with it, where this
			    is 5.9% and 13.3px is already the smallest type on the page. Two
			    consecutive 6% cuts would be the thing to watch, not this one.

			    At 16rem the photo is 304 × 380px, and the LG WIDTH IS NOW THE
			    SMALLEST of the three: 16rem here against the stacked caps of 16rem
			    below sm and 20rem between. That's a first — the lg photo has
			    always been the largest — and it is the signal that this lever is
			    spent. Take the next widening from the gap.

			    --i: 1 so it lands AFTER the card (Hiro, 08-05: the card first,
			    then the image) at the standard 150ms stagger — the SAME beat the
			    home page's grid follows its hero by (Hiro, 08-06: the photo
			    should follow the card at the rate the project cards follow the
			    hero). It was a hand-set --base before: 180ms (in flight before
			    the old five-element cascade's later pieces had started), then
			    450ms against the card-as-one-object — which read right on its
			    own, but priced the page out of the hairline finale: the closing
			    rule fires the frame BOTH index blocks have landed, and a 450ms
			    base pushed the figure's landing well past it, so the close
			    started mid-arrival here. On the shared stagger the two pages'
			    cascades land together and one finale delay is right for both —
			    keep this on the standard beat; a bespoke gap needs a bespoke
			    finale delay to go with it. */}
			<figure class="reveal w-full max-w-[16rem] sm:max-w-[20rem] lg:w-[16rem] lg:max-w-none lg:shrink-0" style="--i: 1">
				<img
					src="/images/me.webp"
					alt="Hiro Eernisse"
					width="832"
					height="1040"
					class="aspect-[4/5] w-full rounded-[var(--radius)] border border-[var(--line)] object-cover"
				/>
				<figcaption class="mt-2 flex items-center gap-1.5 text-[0.7rem] text-[var(--ink-soft)]">
					<svg
						class="h-3 w-3 shrink-0"
						viewBox="0 0 24 24"
						fill="none"
						stroke="currentColor"
						stroke-width="2"
						stroke-linecap="square"
						stroke-linejoin="miter"
						aria-hidden="true"
					>
						<path d="M12 22s7-6.5 7-12a7 7 0 0 0-14 0c0 5.5 7 12 7 12Z" />
						<circle cx="12" cy="10" r="2.5" />
					</svg>
					Eagle Lake, South Lake Tahoe, CA
				</figcaption>
			</figure>
		</div>
	</main>

	{/* Lifted verbatim from the home page (2026-08-05) — same element, same
	    max-w-[960px]/px-5 so the copyright sits on the SAME left edge as the
	    prose and both section rules above it, same py-10, same text-xs on
	    --ink-soft. A copyright line is the lowest-priority text on the site
	    and the home page already settled what that looks like; a second
	    treatment here would be a second answer to a solved question.

	    ⚠ 2026-08-10: THE CONTRAST THIS NOTE WAS DRAWN AGAINST IS GONE. It used
	    to read "deliberately NOT the project pages' footer, which is a back
	    link rather than a copyright" — that one existed because a project page
	    ends several thousand pixels below where you started and needs a way
	    home, where this page ends one screen down with the nav still in reach.
	    The back link was removed that morning and the project pages took this
	    exact footer the same day, so all three routes now end on one line and
	    there is no second kind of footer to be deliberately unlike. The
	    argument still explains why this page never wanted a back link; it no
	    longer explains a difference, because there isn't one.

	    OUTSIDE <main>, matching both. It isn't page content and the reveal
	    cascade doesn't touch it — no .reveal, no --i, so it is simply there
	    when the page is. */}
	<footer class="mx-auto max-w-[960px] px-5 py-10 text-xs text-[var(--ink-soft)]">
		<span>© 2026 Hiro Eernisse</span>
	</footer>
</Layout>
